    ['id' => 'listfilters', 'title' => 'Lists that remember your filters', 'body' => [
      ['ul' => ['Searchable drop-downs: a drop-down with more than eight entries (vessel, company, customer, contact, project, assignee…) opens with a search line — type any part of the name, IMO or code to narrow the list, use ↓ ↑ and Enter to choose, Esc to close. Short lists stay plain drop-downs.']],
      'Every big list — tickets (list and activity tables), offers, equipment, vessels, projects, contacts, companies, timesheets, man-hours, tasks, work orders, approvals, the knowledge base, the Open issues tab of a sub-project and the administrator lists (users, log, customers) — keeps the last filter and sort selection you applied. Coming back to the list from the menu, a dashboard tile or a Back link shows it exactly as you left it, with an amber banner above the filter bar — “Showing your previous selection — items outside these filters are not shown. Press Clear to see everything” — and the Clear button in it.',
      ['fig' => 'g-filters-remembered.jpg', 'cap' => 'Tickets opened again from the menu — the previous selection is back, with the mark next to Clear'],
      ['ul' => ['Apply stores the new selection (a blank field is a choice too). Clear forgets the stored selection and shows the defaults of the list. A link that carries its own filters (a dashboard tile, a vessel\'s “open tickets”) replaces the stored selection with its own.', 'Each list has its own memory, and lists with views (Tickets › Open / All / Activity, Timesheets, Tasks) remember each view separately; the Open issues tab remembers per sub-project. The memory lives in your sign-in session on this device — signing out, or signing in on another device, starts from the defaults again.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'projects', 'title' => 'Projects, sub-projects and the process stages', 'body' => [
      'Time: every date-time in the portal — tickets, notes, report trails, logs, notifications — is shown in the company zone with its abbreviation (EET / EEST), whatever zone the server stores it in (UTC on GoDaddy); pure dates such as deadlines are never shifted. Administrators check and set the two zones in Settings › Numbering › Time zone, where the stored clock and the company clock are shown side by side.',
      'Numbers: new projects and sub-projects are numbered by the portal in the ERP\'s shape — PRJ.000496 and Y.001 — pre-filled on the forms (type over them to use an ERP code); every new offer also gets its SQ number PRJ.000496.Y.001.SQ.0012 next to the document number GAS-OF-…, and proforma invoices will be PRJ.000496.Y.001.PI.0007.n. Administrators set prefixes, digits and the next project number in Settings › Numbering — keep "next project number" above the ERP\'s counter so the two series never meet.',
      'Status — Quoted, Active, On hold, Completed: a project and each of its sub-projects start as Quoted (offer stage) and become Active by themselves the moment one of their offers is approved — Approved, In progress, To be invoiced or Finished (and any custom status that behaves like these). An offer on a sub-project activates that sub-project and the project; an offer on the project as a whole activates the project and the sub-projects that have no offer of their own. If every approval is cancelled or reverted the project goes back to Quoted. On hold and Completed are set by hand and are never changed by offers; the vessel "general" projects (GEN-, service calls without a project) stay active. The Projects list shows Quoted with its own pill and filter (Active + Quoted + On hold by default); the Active pill shows "Active since <date>" on hover, and the offer that activated the project carries the event in its trail. Dashboards, timesheets, tasks and the KPI pages count active projects only.',
      'A main project corresponds to an ERP project code (GY-85-02); its sub-projects are the Y.nn parts (GY-85-02 Y.01 …). Create them manually from Fleet › Projects › + Project / + Sub-project, or load the whole structure from SoftOne through Settings › Import from Excel › Project structures. The project manager named on the project receives notifications for everything that happens in it.',
      ['ul' => ['A project must be linked to a vessel to appear on the vessel page (projects / sub-projects, timeline, services), in the vessel report and in the weekly vessel report; a project without vessel (typical after an import where the vessel name did not match) shows ⚓ No vessel in red in the tree. When the project name points at a registered vessel — by its name, its former name in brackets or an alias — a one-click "Link to M/Y …" appears next to it; otherwise "set the vessel" opens Edit project. Linking also attaches the project\'s tickets that had no vessel.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-projects.jpg', 'cap' => 'The project tree for staff — with edit buttons, offers and roll-ups'],
      ['fig' => 'stages.svg', 'cap' => 'The seven stages — ENG · PROC · PROD · FAT · INST · SAT/HAT · COMM'],
      ['ol' => ['Open the sub-project › Process tab and set the percentage of each stage — tick N/A under a stage that does not exist for this sub-project; press Save. A snapshot is kept so the progress history can be reported.', 'Set the deadline and the invoicing status (none / partial / full, invoiced %) on the sub-project form.', 'Keep the Drawings tab as the drawing register: one line per drawing requested from the yard, owner, maker or Golden A/S, with the date sent, the date expected and the file when it arrives. The sub-project\'s Drawings tab shows the status summary (total, pending, overdue, received, not required), the requests of this sub-project first and then the requests of the whole project that are not linked to a sub-project (the imported Excel tracker) — use ✎ to link a line to the sub-project. The project-level Drawing tracker (project › Drawing tracker) has the same Add a drawing request form (sub-project, system, responsible, dates, remarks, file) plus ✎ edit and a ✓ Received button on every pending line — the owner gets a notification for each new request, and all pending requests appear in the weekly report (Approvals section) and the monthly reports, red when past the expected date, until they are received or set Not required. The Excel import is folded under the form.', 'Submit documents for owner approval from the Owner approval tab, and class submittals from the Class tab, with document number and revision.', 'Record FAT / HAT / SAT with their reports under the test tabs.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-subproject.jpg', 'cap' => 'A sub-project as staff see it — stage editing, offers, and every tab'],
      ['fig' => 'g-weekly-notes.jpg', 'cap' => 'Weekly notes — the progress diary with photos; internal notes stay inside Golden A/S'],
      ['fig' => 'g-files.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Files tab — every file of the sub-project with its archive name, kind and visibility'],
      'Under each expanded project the tree also lists its open tasks (after the offers) and its open tickets — number, subject, status, priority, sub-project, assignee and age, with + New ticket pre-set on the project; the project line carries a gold badge with the count. Every sub-project page shows the tasks of that sub-project on the Process tab — with the Done ring and + New task pre-filled.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'issues', 'title' => 'Open issues per sub-project', 'body' => [
      'Every sub-project has an Open issues tab — the register of what is still pending: the open issues written in the task blocks of the field service reports (one entry per task block, created when the report is submitted and kept in step while the report is edited; reports submitted before the register existed are picked up the first time the tab is opened) and the points the team or the owner\'s side add by hand. Each entry says who has to act (Golden A/S, owner / crew, third party), the assignee, the due date, the priority and the status.',
      ['fig' => 'g-issues.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Open issues tab of a sub-project — register, filters, close with a note'],
      ['ul' => ['Every entry has a permanent number OI-<project code>-nnn, numbered per project and never reused; it is printed in the reports and the export, the search box finds it, and ?p=issue&no=OI-… opens it directly (useful in e-mails and meeting minutes). Entries created before the numbering got theirs the first time the register was opened.']],
      ['ol' => ['Sorting: click a column header to sort the register by that column, click again for the opposite direction (number, issue, source, who acts, assignee, raised, due, status); the order is kept across the pages. Every other table of the portal sorts the same way on click (numbers, dates and text recognised; a paged table sorts the page shown).', 'Add an issue: title, details, who has to act, priority, due date, raised on, assignee and visibility (internal entries never reach the owner).', 'Start marks it in progress; Close asks for a short closing note and records who closed it and when; Re-open brings it back. Overdue entries are shown in red with the badge.', 'Filters: search, status (open + in progress by default) and who has to act; the list is paged.', 'Issues from a service report link back to the report; while the report is still open they follow its text, once closed here they stay closed. Re-opening a report withdraws its still-open entries until it is submitted again.']],
      ['ul' => ['Numbers and categories: every entry carries a permanent number OI-<project code>-nnn (shown in the tab, the reports and the Excel files; the search box finds it) and a category — Mechanical, Electrical, Automation, Navigation & communication, Software / configuration, Documentation / class, Logistics / spare parts, Commercial / offer, Safety, Other — chosen in the form and usable as a filter. Administrators manage the categories in Settings › Issue categories: re-order, re-colour, hide, or add their own (e.g. Hydraulics).', 'Import from Excel / Export: the project manager keeps the list in a workbook — download the template — generated by the portal, so Category, To be done by, Priority, Status, Visibility and Assigned to are drop-downs fed by a Lists sheet with your own categories and staff (No · Issue · Category · Details · To be done by · Priority · Raised · Due · Assigned to · Status · Closed on · Closing note · Visibility) — run Check only, then import: a row whose Issue text already exists in the sub-project updates that entry (columns missing from the sheet keep their values), new texts become manual entries. The Sub-project column (drop-down of the sub-projects of the same main project) sends each row to its sub-project, so one workbook can carry the points of the whole project; an empty cell means the sub-project you import from. Export writes the register in the same layout for a round trip.', 'Reports: the weekly vessel report prints the open issues of every project on board and the ones closed in the week (section 5), the monthly sub-project report has an Open issues section (open + closed in the last 30 days) and the vessel technical history lists the open issues per project. Owners get the external entries only.', 'Planning board: the open items shown while assigning an engineer come from this register.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-issues-weekly.jpg', 'cap' => 'Open issues and closed issues in the weekly vessel report'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'reports', 'title' => 'Reports and issuing documents', 'body' => [
      'Four documents are produced by the portal: the weekly vessel report (GAS-WR-…), the monthly sub-project report (GAS-PR-…), the vessel technical history (GAS-VH-…) and the timesheet statement (GAS-TS-…). The PDF and Word buttons give previews. Issue archives the document with a revision number (R0, R1 …) in the project\'s archive folder and lists it under Issued revisions; what the owner downloads afterwards is that archived file, byte for byte. Field service reports are archived automatically on submit and accept.',
      ['fig' => 'g-reports-tab.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Reports tab of a sub-project with the Issue button and the issued revisions'],
      'Sending a document by e-mail: the ✉ E-mail card at the foot of an offer, a weekly vessel report, a field service / inspection report and an issued works forecast prepares the message — the vessel\'s contacts and the customer\'s portal accounts are listed as recipients in To (tick who gets it, type more addresses), you are in Cc, the subject and a courteous text are pre-filled, and the document(s) are ticked as attachments. Outlook draft downloads an .eml that Outlook opens as a new, editable message with everything inside — check and press Send; Mail app opens your default mail program with recipients and text only (a browser cannot attach files that way), so attach the document yourself. The ✉ E-mail all buttons on the contact lists now put the addresses in To, not Bcc.',
      'E-mail templates (Settings › Lists & categories): administrators write the subject and the text the E-mail card and the E-mail all buttons propose — one template per document kind (offer, weekly report, service report, inspection report, works forecast, E-mail all) plus the signature that closes every message — optionally bound to a language, a branch (the Italian branch signs as its own company) and a customer (a more formal wording for one owner). The most specific active template wins: customer + branch + language, then customer, branch, language, any; the language is the one of the customer\'s portal accounts. Placeholders {no} {title} {vessel} {customer} {project} {week} {doc} {user} {branch} {date} {signature} are filled from the document. The page shows a preview with sample data, the built-in text of each kind, and downloads a test draft to your own address. Without any template the built-in texts of the language files apply, as before.',
      ['ul' => ['Since v1.27.1 the vessel technical history also carries two equipment tables — Equipment on board and Equipment service history — and every serial number has its own service-history document (GAS-EH-…) from the Equipment register (see Equipment register below).']],
      'Standing reminders for every report (weekly on Friday, monthly on the 1st, timesheets on Monday, vessel history, progress updates per sub-project) are created in one go from Work › Tasks › Report reminders; they arrive in the bell of the project manager or the assignee.',
      ['fig' => 'g-weekly.jpg', 'cap' => 'The weekly vessel report page — week navigation, issue, revisions'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'colours', 'title' => 'Colour code in the reports', 'body' => [
      'Since v1.29.6 the weekly vessel report, the vessel technical history, the monthly sub-project report, the inspection reports and the equipment service history use one colour code wherever the reader\'s attention is needed, and every document explains it in a legend under its title. A narrow coloured bar at the left of a table row marks the state of that row; the figure that caused it (a due date, a number of days, a delay, a risk) is printed in the same colour. Rows without a state keep no bar, so a clean report stays quiet.',
      ['fig' => 'g-colour-code.jpg', 'cap' => 'The weekly vessel report with the legend under the title and the bars at the left of the rows'],
      ['ul' => ['Red — overdue, failed, high or critical risk, rejected, a delay flag, an offer pending longer than its validity, a ticket waiting too long.', 'Amber (gold) — due within 7 days, pending or waiting, medium risk, drafts, offers waiting for an answer.', 'Green — done, on time, accepted, closed, low or no risk, progress gained during the week.', 'Navy — in progress, no attention needed.', 'Grey — not applicable.']],
      ['ul' => ['The field service report keeps its own layout; only its Open issues box takes the accent (amber, red when the owner disputed the report). The Word versions carry the same bars in a narrow first column and the same coloured figures, so the colours survive editing.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'tickets', 'title' => 'Tickets', 'body' => [
      'Work › Tickets shows every ticket across the fleet with filters (status, vessel, customer, assignee, category, priority, age) and a summary strip: aging of open tickets, new and unassigned, resolution time, activity per engineer and per customer. Unassigned and overdue tickets appear in your attention list.',
      ['ul' => ['The KPIs, the aging chart and the two activity tables live on the 📊 Reports tab of Work › Tickets (the list tabs stay lists). Every number in the activity tables is a link to the matching ticket list — an engineer\'s open, waiting or resolved tickets; a customer\'s raised, last-90-days, open, waiting or resolved tickets — so a figure is one click from the tickets behind it. The category Customer complaint marks quality or service complaints: the Reports tab counts the open ones (red when any) and the list filters by category.', 'The filter bar above the KPIs drives the two activity tables (per Golden A/S staff, per customer): name search, the period in which the tickets were raised (last 30 / 90 / 365 days, this year, last year or a custom range), "only with open tickets" and the sort column; both tables are paged (25 / 50 / 100). The KPIs stay global.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-tickets-activity.jpg', 'cap' => 'Tickets — activity filters, KPIs and the activity tables'],
      ['fig' => 'g-tickets.jpg', 'cap' => 'The ticket list for staff'],
      ['fig' => 'flow-ticket.svg', 'cap' => 'The ticket lifecycle'],
      ['ul' => ['Assigned to filter: — any — shows every ticket; Unassigned is an explicit entry (the “New, unassigned” tile uses it). Project filter: a ticket raised as generic support has no project and is not listed under a project filter — place it under the project first.', 'The ticket form says in red when the chosen vessel has no project linked to it in the portal (a project imported without a vessel, or a duplicate vessel record — see One record per vessel), with a link to Fleet › Projects.']],
      ['ol' => ['Assign the ticket to an engineer (or yourself) and set the status to In progress.', 'Place the ticket under a project / sub-project when it belongs to one; it then appears in the vessel and project history.', 'Use internal comments for Golden A/S-only remarks and external ones to talk to the owner; set Waiting for owner when you need an answer.', 'Create a work order or a service report from the ticket; they stay linked.', 'Write the resolution and set Resolved; close after the owner\'s acceptance. Administrators can delete a ticket in any status.']],
      ['ul' => ['Bulk actions on the ticket list (Work › Tickets): tick the tickets — or "All n matching the filters" for the whole filtered list across pages — choose Close, Re-open or Delete (administrators only) in the "with the selection" box and Apply. Closing writes the usual status event on every ticket; deleting removes the ticket with its comments, events and files for good (work orders and service reports that pointed at it are kept, unlinked). Filter first (e.g. status Resolved, older than a date) so the selection is exactly what you mean.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-ticket.jpg', 'cap' => 'A ticket for staff — assignment, project placement, status actions, internal / external comments'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'fd', 'title' => 'Ticket history from Freshdesk', 'body' => [
      'Settings › Freshdesk import brings the tickets of the old Freshdesk helpdesk into the portal so that the history of every vessel is in one place. Copy the export files (Tickets0.xml, Tickets1.xml …, 20–100 MB each) by FTP into customer/storage/import/freshdesk/ — small files can be uploaded from the page — and press Check on a file: nothing is written, you get the mapping report (vessels found / missing, values that are not a vessel, agents matched to portal accounts, statuses, types, Prj No codes). Then Import: the page processes 100 tickets per step and reloads by itself until the file is done; a file can be re-run at any time, tickets already imported are skipped.',
      ['fig' => 'g-fd-import.jpg', 'cap' => 'Settings › Freshdesk import — files, check result and progress'],
      ['ul' => ['Imported tickets keep their Freshdesk number as FD-nnnn, their dates, requester (raised by, with the side taken from the e-mail domain), agent (assigned, matched to a portal staff account by name), status, priority and type; the first lines of the description say where the ticket came from and keep Important notes, Deadline, Service date, S/N, Warranty, tags and the attachment names (the files themselves stay in Freshdesk).', 'Every reply, note, forwarded mail, form and summary becomes a comment with its author and date; private Freshdesk notes are internal comments, so owners never see them. Automated mails and system entries are left out; deleted and spam tickets are skipped.', 'Vessels: the Vessel field is matched to the fleet ignoring M/Y, S/Y, P/S prefixes and "(ex NAME)" parts; with "create missing vessels" ticked the rest are created without a customer — assign them afterwards in Fleet › Vessels so the owners see their history. Values such as Unknown, IT Tickets or a project code are kept as text and the ticket has no vessel (owners do not see it). Prj No (PRJ.000064.Y.002) places the ticket under the project and sub-project when they exist.', 'S/N: a serial number found in the equipment register writes a ticket record into the unit\'s service history.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-fd-ticket.jpg', 'cap' => 'An imported ticket — origin line, kept fields and the conversation as comments'],
      ['ul' => ['Tickets that arrived without a vessel (no match in the old helpdesk data) show as generic support with no vessel. On the ticket page the Project assignment card now has a Vessel field: choose the vessel, then the project and sub-project, and save — the ticket takes the vessel\'s customer and appears in the vessel history. The same card re-places any ticket on another vessel if it was raised on the wrong one.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'joborders', 'title' => 'Work orders', 'body' => [
      'Work orders are numbered per project (GY-85-02-JO-0001-YYYYMMDD) and carry a description, location, date and priority. Golden A/S requests them for owner approval, or approves the ones owners request.',
      ['fig' => 'g-joborders.jpg', 'cap' => 'The work-order list'],
      ['fig' => 'flow-jo.svg', 'cap' => 'Draft → pending approval → approved → in progress → done'],
      ['fig' => 'g-jo.jpg', 'cap' => 'A work order — approval, start, finish, linked service report and history'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'fsr', 'title' => 'Field service reports (SF-GLD-014)', 'body' => [
      'Hour types on the time lines: WT working time, TT travelling time, and NC not chargeable — hours worked that are not billed to the customer (warranty, goodwill, rework, internal). NC hours stay on the report and in the hours summary (own column; the total includes them) but never reach the accounting register, so they are not invoiced. The engineer picks the type on the form; management can correct it while the report is a draft or disputed.',
      ['ul' => ['Service calls without a project: on the vessel page press + Service report — the portal form opens and files the report under the vessel\'s general project (GEN-<vessel>), so timesheets, the register and accounting work as for any project. The owner sees it under the vessel\'s Services tab.', 'External reports: a report written outside the portal (yard form, handwritten sheet, maker\'s report) is attached with 📎 External service report (vessel page or a project\'s report list): date, location, engineers, the file (PDF / photo / Word / Excel), a few lines on what it says, and whether the owner sees it. It gets a number of its own — EXT-YYYYMMDD-INI-n — an External pill and, until processed, a Not processed pill. Its hours are not in the accounting register. Filed on the wrong project, or on the vessel in general? Open it and use 🔗 Link to a project / sub-project — the report and its file move there (logged); a converted internal report is not touched.', 'Processing an external report: open it and press ⇄ Create internal report — the SF-GLD-014 form opens pre-filled (vessel, date, location, PIC) with the external file as evidence; type the tasks and the hour lines, submit; the two reports are linked both ways (the internal PDF says "from EXT-…"), and only the internal one reaches accounting. The Accounting page lists the external reports still not processed so nothing is forgotten.']],
      'Work › Service Reports is the FSR register. An engineer creates a report for a project (and sub-project), fills the report data, the persons in charge, the tasks with start / end / type (travelling or working time) and spare parts, the open issues and photos per task, saves drafts as often as needed and submits. Submitting generates the PDF and the Word document, archives them under the report number and notifies the owner for acceptance.',
      ['fig' => 'g-fsr-list.jpg', 'cap' => 'The service-report list — draft, submitted, accepted, disputed'],
      ['fig' => 'flow-fsr.svg', 'cap' => 'Draft → submitted → accepted / disputed → re-opened'],
      ['ul' => ['Report numbers are yyyymmdd + engineer initials + time (20261009IB0949); each resubmission increases the version (R1, R2 …) and both files are regenerated.', 'A disputed report is re-opened, corrected and submitted again; the owner\'s remark stays in the history.', 'Link the report to the ticket or work order it answers; it is then shown on both.', 'Administrators can delete a report in any status (files included).']],
      ['fig' => 'g-fsr.jpg', 'cap' => 'A submitted and accepted service report'],
      ['ul' => ['Equipment serviced: tick the units of the vessel the report concerns (or type new serial numbers to create them). On submission the tasks, open issues, parts and engineers are written to the service history of every ticked unit; re-opening removes the entries until the report is submitted again — see Equipment register.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-fsr.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Equipment serviced box of a field service report'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'branches', 'title' => 'Branches — head office and the Italian branch', 'body' => [
      'The group runs projects from two companies: the head office in Greece (ΣΥΣΤΗΜΑΤΑ ΙΣΧΥΟΣ ΤΕΧΝΙΚΗ ΚΑΙ ΕΜΠΟΡΙΚΗ ΜΟΝΟΠΡΟΣΩΠΗ Α.Ε., Keratsini) and the Italian branch (GOLDEN AS ITALIAN, Massarosa (LU), P.IVA IT02768900462). Settings › Branches holds their legal identity, document prefix (GAS / GIT), offer series, default working calendar and logo variant. Customers, vessels, contacts, systems, disciplines, task types, file categories, calendars and the licence stay group-wide.',
      ['fig' => 'g-branches.jpg', 'cap' => 'Settings › Branches — the two companies of the group'],
      ['ol' => ['Set the branch on the project form; every sub-project inherits it unless its own form sets another one. Everything below — weekly and monthly reports, vessel histories, timesheet statements, service and inspection reports, offers, tickets, work orders, tasks and files — follows the sub-project and is never assigned a branch of its own.', 'Documents issued under an Italian sub-project carry the branch\'s trade name and P.IVA in the footer, the logo with the ITALIAN BRANCH line and the GIT prefix in their numbers (GIT-WR-…, GIT-PR-…, GIT-TS-…, GIT-VH-…); service and inspection report numbers stay portal-wide. The archive tree does not change.', 'Every offer created in the portal gets its number at creation from the series of the issuing branch — GAS-OF-2026-001 for the head office, GIT-OF-2026-001 for the Italian branch (Settings › Branches › Offer series; empty = document prefix + OF). Offers loaded from the ERP (Settings › Import from Excel › Offers) keep the SoftOne number and carry a grey ERP pill in the offer list and on the offer page.', 'Mean manhour cost (Settings › Branches, default 35 €/h): the company\'s cost of one labour hour for the branch. Budgets, actual costs, the cost-control board, KPIs (BAI, ROI, CPI), the accounting-report cost column and the Manhours report use the discipline\'s own cost rate (Settings › Disciplines) and fall back to this figure when the discipline has none.', 'Give each staff account a home branch (Portal accounts). Branch scope is off by default — everyone sees the whole group; tick it to limit an account to its own branch\'s projects. Administrators are always group-wide.', 'The Manhours report adds a By branch table: hours by the branch that runs the work, with the home branch of the engineers on the second line — intra-group work shows where the two differ. Charge rates are the same for all branches.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-project-branch.jpg', 'cap' => 'The project form — Branch field; sub-projects inherit it'],
      ['ul' => ['Each branch can upload its own logo for the documents (Settings › Branches › Edit › Logo on the documents: PNG or JPEG, wide format, up to 2 MB). The uploaded logo replaces the built-in variant on every PDF and Word document the branch issues and is kept under storage/, so portal updates never overwrite it; remove it to return to the built-in variant.', 'Pills on the project list and the sub-project page show the branch (navy GR, gold IT); the project list filters by branch.', 'A branch in use cannot be deleted — hide it. The default branch (head office) covers every project without a branch of its own.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'isr', 'title' => 'Inspection reports (SF-GLD-020)', 'body' => [
      'An inspection report records a condition survey of the vessel\'s systems. Open it from the sub-project › Service reports tab (+ New inspection report) or from the project list (Inspection reports). The cover carries the report data of the form — date, inspector, customer, person in charge, vessel, location, class / flag, builder / year, hull number and subject — followed by the three introductory texts: subject and objective, main findings and recommendations, risks and hazards.',
      ['fig' => 'flow-isr.svg', 'cap' => 'Draft → under check → issued; re-open for corrections'],
      ['ol' => ['Add system: pick the system from the list (the vessel-systems list plus the sections of the SF-GLD-020 template) — the checklist is filled from the template\'s question bank — or choose Other… and describe the item yourself.', 'Fill in the condition of the equipment as found, answer the checklist (edit or remove questions, add your own; Fill from template adds the missing template questions), the class regulations if applicable, the risk level with its analysis and the proposed corrective actions.', 'Attach photos, PDFs or drawings with a caption; images are numbered as figures in the report, in the order of the systems.', 'Save as draft as often as needed, then Submit for check. A colleague or an administrator reads the report and presses Check & issue — the author cannot issue his own report — or re-opens it for corrections.', 'Issuing generates the PDF and the Word document, archives them under inspections/ with revision R[n] and shows the report to the owner on Work › Service Reports and on the sub-project tab. Re-opening an issued report and issuing it again produces the next revision.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-isr-edit.jpg', 'cap' => 'A system block — system picker, condition as found, checklist from the template, class regulations, risk analysis and level, corrective actions, attachments'],
      ['ul' => ['Report numbers are ISR-yyyymmdd-<initials>-nn (ISR-20261018-IB-01); attachments are filed as <report no>_<nn>_sys<n>.<ext>, the documents as <report no>_R<n>.pdf / .docx.', 'The risk level of each system (low / medium / high / critical) is shown on the list as the highest risk of the report, so the critical surveys stand out.', 'Reports under check and issued ones raise notifications for the project staff; the owner is notified when a report is issued.', 'Administrators can delete a report in any status (attachments and documents included).']],
      ['fig' => 'g-isr.jpg', 'cap' => 'An issued inspection report — report data, introduction, inspected systems with their risk levels'],
      ['ul' => ['Equipment inspected: tick the units of the vessel the survey concerns. Issuing the report writes one inspection record per unit (system, condition found, risk, corrective actions) into its service history; re-opening removes it. The owner sees the record once the report is issued.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-isr.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Equipment inspected box of an inspection report'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'teams', 'title' => 'Sub-project teams — who works on what', 'body' => [
      'Each sub-project has a Team box on its Process tab: the project manager or an administrator ticks the Golden A/S engineers who work on it (grouped by discipline; the project manager is always in). An engineer then finds in the timesheet, field service report and inspection report forms only the active sub-projects he is assigned to; a sub-project of a completed project, or a completed sub-project, drops out by itself. The dashboard lists My sub-projects.',
      ['fig' => 'g-team.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Team box on a sub-project — edit mode, engineers grouped by discipline'],
      ['ul' => ['Project managers see every active sub-project of their own projects; administrators see everything.', 'The limit is enforced on save, not only in the lists: a sub-project that is not assigned is refused with "This sub-project is not assigned to you".', 'Settings › Portal accounts › "Limit every engineer to assigned sub-projects": on, everyone is limited (an engineer with no assignment sees nothing until assigned); off — the open mode — only engineers who already have at least one assignment are limited, so teams can be set up gradually.', 'The accounts export carries an Assigned sub-projects column ("GY-85-02 Y.01, GY-85-02 Y.03"); the same column in the import replaces a person\'s assignments in bulk.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-my-subs.jpg', 'cap' => 'An engineer\'s dashboard — My sub-projects'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'planning', 'title' => 'Planning board — who is where, day by day', 'body' => [
      'Work › Planning is the yearly planning matrix inside the portal: engineers in rows grouped by discipline, the days of the month in columns, weekends and the holidays of the working calendars greyed. A cell holds what the engineer does that day — a sub-project (one colour per vessel), a project when the sub-project is not known yet, or a status: leave, sick, parental leave, strike, office, warehouse, training, travel. An engineer may carry several assignments on the same day (two vessels, two sub-projects). Unplanned working days ahead are hatched so gaps stand out; the Totals columns count work, leave and sick days and list the vessels of the month.',
      ['fig' => 'g-planning.jpg', 'cap' => 'The planning board — one month, engineers by discipline, one colour per vessel'],
      ['ol' => ['Click an empty cell (or the + next to a name, or + Assign) — the editor opens with the engineer and the day; set the range From / To, what (sub-project, project or status), optionally a car and a note, and Save. Weekends and holidays are skipped unless "Include weekends and holidays" is ticked; "Replace" clears whatever was planned on those days first.', 'While you choose a sub-project the editor lists the open items of its previous reports — the open issues of the service reports and the corrective actions of the issued inspection reports — so the engineer leaves with the right spares and the right list.', 'Click a chip to see its details and open items, or delete it; "Clear the days" removes everything planned for the engineer in the range.', 'A sub-project assignment puts the engineer in that sub-project\'s team automatically, so the sub-project is offered in his timesheet, service report and inspection report forms.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-planning-editor.jpg', 'cap' => 'The assignment editor — range, sub-project, car, note and the open items of the previous reports'],
      ['ul' => ['Who plans: administrators, accounts with the Project Manager role and the project managers of active projects; every other engineer opens My planning — his own row, with this and next week in detail (assignment, car, note, open items) and a My week card on the dashboard.', 'Settings › Disciplines › Planning board: tick the disciplines whose engineers appear on the board (and are matched by the Excel import); nothing ticked = everybody. "No discipline" covers accounts without a discipline.', 'Cars (administrators, at the bottom of the page): name, plate, remarks; an assignment carries at most one car.', 'Template: Export year is the import template — every day cell has a drop-down with the choices of the day it was exported (statuses, vessels with an active project, active project codes), the Lists sheet carries the cars and the filling rules ("MIZU / BASH IV" for two assignments, "MIZU + Car 1 (ABC-1234)" for a car); download it again when projects, vessels or cars change. ', 'Import Excel matrix loads the yearly planning file (names in column B, dates in row 3, cells = vessel or ADEIA / SICK / PARENTAL / APERGIA / warehouse / GOLDEN): names are matched in any order, Greek or Latin, vessels to their active project, "MIZU / BASH IV" becomes two assignments, unknown cells are kept as text so nothing is lost. Always "Check only" first; the report lists names and codes that did not match. Export year gives the same matrix back with the counters.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-planning-discs.jpg', 'cap' => 'Settings › Disciplines — which disciplines take part in the planning'],
      ['fig' => 'g-planning-me.jpg', 'cap' => 'My planning — an engineer\'s own view with the next two weeks and the open items'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'timesheets', 'title' => 'Timesheets and man-hours', 'body' => [
      'Work › Timesheets is where engineers book hours per project and period (normal, overtime, holiday), submit them to their manager, and where managers approve or reject. Since v1.23.0 the working calendar of the engineer reclassifies hours on save — holidays, Sundays and hours beyond the daily normal become holiday or overtime automatically (see Working hours below). Administrators mark approved timesheets as invoiced with the invoice reference. Man-hours summarises hours per project, vessel, discipline and period, exports CSV and issues the timesheet statement.',
      ['fig' => 'g-timesheets.jpg', 'cap' => 'The timesheet list with the to-approve badge'],
      ['fig' => 'flow-timesheet.svg', 'cap' => 'Draft → submitted → approved / rejected → invoiced'],
      ['fig' => 'g-manhours.jpg', 'cap' => 'Man-hours per project with the report buttons and the issued statements'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'works', 'title' => 'Work packages — assigning the works of a sub-project to engineers', 'body' => [
      'The Works tab of a sub-project (v1.36.0) breaks the work into packages — W01, W02 … with a title, description, discipline, working area on the deck, planned hours, start and end, priority, the engineers (one lead) — and assigns them. Saving writes the engineers\' planning automatically: one entry per engineer and workday between start and end, the hours split evenly, leave / sick / training days left untouched (a warning names anyone who had no free day in the period). Status: Planned (nobody) → Assigned → In progress (engineer) → Done (engineer, frees the remaining planned days) → Checked (PM, managers, administrators); Cancelled also frees the days.',
      ['ol' => ['Who assigns: administrators, the project\'s PM and the planning editors (managers). The assignment form shows next to every engineer his planned load of the next two weeks and his first free day, grouped by discipline.', 'The engineer: Work › My works lists his packages with this week\'s planning; the bell carries "Work assigned to you"; Acknowledge tells the PM it was seen; Start and Mark done are one click there or on the package page; Book hours opens a timesheet.', 'Timesheets: every line may name the package (the list follows the chosen sub-project); Fill from the plan pre-fills a new sheet with the planned days, sub-projects, packages and hours of the week — the engineer corrects and submits. Approved hours land on the package (plan / booked), on the engineer and on the discipline budget.', 'Planning: the entries written by a package carry W01 and its title in the chip; Done / Cancelled frees the days after today; editing the dates or the people of a package rewrites its entries. Right-click any chip (administrators and planning editors) for Delete this entry, Delete the whole package planning (all days — the package stays, its automatic planning is switched off) and Open.', 'Weekly works forecast: when the draft of a week is opened, the packages of that week that have a working area are added as Plan A items (area, description, days, sub-project) — once per area.', 'Budget: the Works tab shows planned hours against the discipline budget of the sub-project and warns when the packages exceed it.']],
      'Work › Workload (managers, administrators, roles with financial data): capacity against planned work per discipline and per engineer for 4–12 weeks — a bar per week (green < 85 %, gold 85–100 %, red over, hatched = leave / sick / training / travel), the projects and sub-projects he is planned on (each chip opens the project tree or the sub-project\'s Works tab), his open packages with the remaining hours, the next free day and the free days in the next 8 weeks. Use it before assigning: the same figures appear next to each name in the assignment form.',
      ['tip' => 'Keep the working calendars and the engineers\' disciplines current (Settings › Working hours, Portal accounts): capacity, the free days and the discipline grouping come from them.'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'budget', 'title' => 'Budget vs actual — manhours and parts per sub-project', 'body' => [
      'Internal, for Golden A/S roles whose financial data is visible (v1.35.0). Each sub-project carries a budget — manhours per discipline (the Disciplines list with their rates, snapshotted on the budget) and a parts budget in €, saved as revisions R0, R1 … by the project manager, managers or administrators — and the portal compares it live with the actuals: approved / invoiced timesheet hours of the sub-project grouped by the engineer\'s discipline and costed at the discipline rates per hour type, plus the parts cost entries (orders = committed, invoices = invoiced to us). The result is offer value (the approved offers of the sub-project, or a value typed on the budget) minus hours cost and parts; the forecast at completion adds the remaining budgeted hours and keeps the larger of parts budget / committed; earned hours are the budget × stage progress, drawn as a gold tick on the hours bar.',
      ['ol' => ['The live strip under the sub-project title, on every tab: result to date and forecast, hours bar with the progress tick, parts bar, offer value and cost to date, the flags (Hours over budget, Hours ahead of progress, Parts over budget, Forecast loss) and when the figures last moved. Budget & actual › opens the tab.', 'The Budget & actual tab: four tiles, Manhours per discipline (budget, actual, remaining, bar), Parts and materials (budget / committed / invoiced, the cost entries with PO or invoice attached, + Add cost entry), the Result build-up (offer value → hours cost → parts → cost to date → forecast → budgeted vs forecast margin), the € bars, and Edit budget (hours and rate per discipline, parts budget, offer value override, remarks; the first revision is pre-filled from the offer lines — rate card A/B/C/H/N → Service, D/J/K → Automation, E → Engineering, L/M → Electrical, I → NavCom) with the revision history.', 'Cost control (Projects › € Cost control on each project): five project tiles, one row per sub-project with twin bars (grey budget, blue actual, red when over, gold progress tick), result, forecast and status pill; click a row for its discipline breakdown; the cumulative cost curve (budget phased over the project period vs approved hours × rate + cost entries); ⤓ Cost report PDF (GAS-CC-… internal document with the same tables).', 'Elsewhere: the result pill per sub-project in the project tree (green / gold / red), "over budget" sub-projects in the dashboard attention list, and a notification to the PM and administrators when hours or parts exceed the budget (sent when a timesheet is approved or a cost entry saved).']],
      ['tip' => 'Never shown to the owner, never in the owner\'s reports. Keep the Disciplines rates current (Settings › Disciplines) — they cost the actual hours; the budget keeps the rate of the day it was saved.'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'offers', 'title' => 'Offers and offer statuses', 'body' => [
      'Commercial › Offers lists every offer with number, revision, subject, amount, status and aging; offers are shown on top of their project in the tree and on the sub-project page, with the offer document for download. Owners approve or reject from their side; staff set the later statuses (In progress, To be invoiced, Finished, or any status the administrator defined in Settings › Offer statuses).',
      ['fig' => 'g-offers.jpg', 'cap' => 'The offer list for staff'],
      'Building an offer in the portal (v1.34.0): + New offer — from the Offers list, a vessel page (+ New offer) or a ticket (€ Offer) — asks for the vessel and, when there is one, the project, sub-project or ticket; "vessel in general" files the offer under the vessel\'s general project (created on first use). Save it as a draft: the owner does not see drafts. On the offer page the Lines card prices the offer from the rate cards of the branch (Settings › Rate cards): pick the rate card (A Service engineer, D Automation programmer, N Remote support …) and the line, local or overseas zone, quantity and discount — the unit price comes from the price list and can be overridden; free lines take parts and lump sums, heading lines group the table. Quantities, prices, discounts and the VAT % are edited in the table and saved together; ↑ ↓ reorder, × removes; the amount of the offer is the net total of the lines. The Texts card holds the introduction, scope, exclusions, validity, prices and payment, terms and closing, copied from the templates (Settings › Offer texts) in the owner\'s language and edited freely on the offer; Reset from templates starts again. The Document card previews the PDF and Word at any time; Issue R0 archives both as the first revision, sets the PDF as the offer document and marks the offer Sent; later Issues add R1, R2 … while the owner\'s approval flow, the notes, the tasks and the E-mail card work as before.',
      ['ul' => ['Who may build offers: Golden A/S roles with "May prepare offers" (Settings › Golden A/S roles; on by default) whose financial data is visible; administrators always. Everybody else sees offers as before.', 'Layouts: Service offer, System offer, Remote support plan — they change the default scope text and let the templates differ per layout.', 'Rate cards: Settings › Lists & categories › Rate cards imports the master file GAS-PL-<year>_R<n>-service-rates.xlsx (one price column pair per branch); a new revision supersedes the previous list of the branch, offers keep the list they were priced from, and a branch without its own prices uses the GAS list.']],
      'E-mailing the offer: the ✉ E-mail card at the foot of the offer page lists the vessel\'s contacts and portal accounts as recipients in To, pre-fills subject and text and attaches the offer document — Outlook draft (.eml with the attachment) or Mail app (no attachment); see Reports and issuing documents.',
      ['tip' => 'Offers are visible only to owners and to Golden A/S staff whose role shows financial data (Settings › Golden A/S roles); every other staff member works without the offers module — see Roles and access.'],
      ['ul' => ['Internal notes (v1.28.0): the right-hand card of an offer is a Golden A/S-only thread — calls, negotiation, who promised what, follow-ups — written while the owner is deciding or later; the owner never sees it, unlike the comments typed with a status change, which go into the trail. Every new note notifies the project manager and the administrators; authors edit or delete their own notes, administrators any.', 'Tasks on an offer: the Tasks card lists the tasks linked to the offer with their due date, assignee and status; + New task opens a task already on the offer (project and sub-project pre-set). In the task form the Offer field lists the offers of the chosen project; a task shows its offer in the task lists.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-offer-notes.jpg', 'cap' => 'An offer with the internal notes thread and its tasks'],
      ['fig' => 'g-task-offer.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Offer field of a task'],
      ['fig' => 'flow-offer.svg', 'cap' => 'Offer statuses'],
      ['ul' => ['+ Offer: project, sub-project, offer number (filled in by the portal: <branch series>-<year>-<number>, e.g. GAS-OF-2026-001; type over it only to record an external number), revision, subject, amount and currency, dates and the PDF.',
        'Budgetary offers (release D2): an offer of kind Budgetary sits on the main project without a sub-project and carries an AACE 18R-97 estimate class (Settings › Offer classes & payment terms: Class 5 order of magnitude … Class 1 firm, with definition maturity and expected accuracy, editable); the class prints with the offer and shows as a pill; owners see and approve budgetary offers like any other. Once approved, Sales converts it — Convert to firm offer, choose the sub-project — into a firm draft offer (new number and SQ, lines, texts and payment terms copied, both offers linked).',
        'Payment terms and proforma invoices: every offer carries its instalments (% of the net amount or a fixed amount, trigger, days until due; a default scheme per branch is proposed). Once the offer is approved each instalment is a proforma slot: Sales (Portal accounts › Sales) presses + Proforma — the draft gets the number PRJ.000496.Y.001.PI.0007.n, the invoicing identity of the sub-project (or the vessel\'s default), its VAT %, amount and due date from the term; check it, upload the ERP\'s proforma PDF if you prefer it to the portal\'s, Issue (PDF generated, number final), ✉ E-mail it (status Sent). Accounting links the invoices that settle it (number, date, amount, paid amount and date) on the proforma page; the proforma turns Partly paid / Paid from them. Commercial › Proformas lists everything with open, overdue, partly paid and paid counters, filters and totals.',
        'Financial status: offered (approved offers), invoiced (proformas issued), collected (paid), outstanding and overdue are shown as pills on the project tree, on the sub-project head and under the payment terms of an offer — for roles with financial visibility.',
        'Cash flow (Portal accounts › Cash flow permission): every project has 💰 Cash flow on the project tree and Commercial › Cash flow shows the company (filters by branch, vessel, PM; completed projects optional), by month or by week, three months back and nine ahead. In: paid accounting invoices (by payment date), the open part of issued proformas (by due date, overdue counted now), the instalments of approved offers not yet issued (approval date + due days). Out: approved hours × cost (discipline rate or branch mean), parts invoiced and ordered (Budget & actual cost entries), subcontracting lines with a PO (delivered by delivery date, otherwise by due date), and the remaining budgeted hours × average rate spread to the deadline. Chart (in above / out below the axis, cumulative line, current period marked), per-period table with the main items, sources breakdown, PDF GAS-CF-YYYY-MM and Excel with a Drivers sheet.', 'Settings › Import from Excel › Offers loads or updates offers in bulk from the SoftOne export.', 'Amounts are masked (•••) for Golden A/S roles without financial visibility.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'kpis', 'title' => 'Project-management KPIs, risk log and sales KPIs', 'body' => [
      'Work › KPIs computes the management KPIs live from the portal\'s own data, per project and per sub-project, for a period (this month, last month, quarter, year to date, 12 months): PCR (sub-projects completed on time ÷ due in the period), PCRt (budgeted ÷ actual manhours), BAI-b (internal budget ÷ actual cost) and BAI-o (client offer value ÷ actual cost), RUIs / RUIa (budgeted ÷ actual hours of service and assistant engineers, by rate card), PSC (drawing-tracker revisions + re-submitted owner / class documents — lower is better), RMI (mitigated ÷ identified risks), ROI (result ÷ cost to date), CPI (earned ÷ actual hours) and SPI (earned ÷ planned hours to date — from the work packages, else linear to the deadline). Green at or above the target, amber within the tolerance (default 0.9 – 1), red below; a dash means not enough data (no budget, no hours, no risks).',
      ['ul' => ['Targets and tolerances per KPI are set under Settings › KPI targets, with "notify the PM on red": the first visit of each month stores a snapshot of every project, and a KPI that turns red since the previous snapshot sends a bell notification to the project manager. The snapshots draw the trend sparklines on the KPI page.', 'The dashboard\'s KPI cockpit lists the five projects with most red / amber KPIs this month. ⤓ PDF issues the KPI report (GAS-KPI-YYYY-MM) and ⤓ Excel the same figures per project and sub-project.', 'What feeds the KPIs: the sub-project deadline and the date it was set Completed (PCR); Budget & actual (PCRt, BAI, CPI, SPI, ROI); the rate card of each person — Settings › Portal accounts, else the discipline\'s card — for RUIs (cards A, C, D, E, I, L) and RUIa (B, M); the Drawing tracker revisions and the Owner approval / Class re-submissions (PSC); the risk log (RMI).']],
      'Risk log: every project has one (project tree › ⚠ Risk log; sub-project › Risks tab): number RK-001…, title, category, likelihood × impact (1–5 each, score coloured), owner, due date, mitigation, status Open / Mitigated / Accepted / Occurred / Closed. ⇣ From inspection reports pulls every item of the issued inspection reports that carries a risk level into the log once, so the initial vessel inspection seeds it — the inspectors own the first entries.',
      'Commercial › Sales KPIs (financial roles): hit rate by count and value, pipeline value by aging (≤ 7, ≤ 14, > 14 days), weighted forecast (open offers × the probability of their status, Settings › Sales targets), average decision time and offers decided after their validity, quote turnaround (requested date → sent; the Requested on field on the offer, defaulting to the ticket date), average order value, offered → invoiced (from the accounting register), discounts given, repeat customers (≥ 2 won in 12 months) and the share of the top 5, service calls that got an offer within 30 days; tables per sales person (the offer\'s creator) and per customer, this month vs the targets, why offers were lost (the reason asked when an offer is rejected). ⤓ PDF issues the sales report (GAS-SR-YYYY-MM), ⤓ Excel the tables.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'accounting', 'title' => 'Accounting register and accounting reports', 'body' => [
      'Commercial › Accounting is where billable hours become invoices. The register holds every approved timesheet line and every submitted service-report hour line of the company once, with an accounting state: Open → In a draft report → With accounting → Invoiced, or Excluded (the PM decided not to invoice it, with a reason). A line that is with accounting or invoiced can never enter another report — so the same hours are never billed twice, whoever prepares the next report.',
      ['ol' => ['New accounting report: pick the scope — a vessel, or a project / sub-project of it — the period and the rate zone (local / outside Attica & overseas); every open line in that scope joins the report and is priced from the current rate card of the branch: the discipline\'s card (Settings › Disciplines › Rate card, auto by name otherwise), the hour type (normal, overtime, holiday / weekend, travel) and the zone.', 'Review: the lines are grouped per project › sub-project › discipline with the budgeted hours, the hours already invoiced and the hours in this report (red when the sub-project goes over budget). For every line choose Include or Exclude (with a reason), set a discount per line, and if needed another rate card line or zone; a header discount applies to the whole report. Save the review — the lines are repriced — until everything is classified.', 'Issue: the report gets its number (GAS-AR-2026-001 / GIT-AR-… per branch, R0), the PDF and the Excel are archived under the project, the included lines go "with accounting" and the timesheets and service reports concerned show "Sent to accounting".', 'Send: the ✉ E-mail card under the report has the branch\'s accounting address (Settings › Branches › Accounting e-mail) in To and both files attached; the download of the .eml marks the report as sent.', 'Accounting answers in the portal (accounts ticked "Accounting" on Portal accounts, and administrators): Invoiced with the invoice number and date — every line and the source documents become Invoiced; Partly invoiced — tick the invoiced lines in the € column first; Returned with a note — the report goes back to the project manager, who corrects and issues R1. The PM is notified of every answer.']],
      ['ul' => ['Nobody has to hunt for open hours: the Accounting page opens with "Waiting to be reported" — every vessel (or project without vessel) that has approved hours not yet in a report, oldest first, red after 30 days, with the number of timesheet and service-report lines. "+ Collect" makes the draft for that vessel; "▶ Weekly round — one draft per vessel" makes one draft for every vessel in the list in one go (Friday routine), each to be reviewed and issued separately. Parts and other costs are not hours: they stay in Budget & actual / Cost control and in the offers.', 'Who prepares: administrators, the project manager of the project (for his projects) and every account ticked "May prepare accounting reports" on Portal accounts.', 'The register (Commercial › Accounting › Register) filters by vessel, project, discipline, engineer, state, hour type and period, totals hours and value, and exports to Excel; ↻ Refresh registers lines that arrived since the page was opened (it also runs automatically when the pages open).', 'A draft can be deleted — its lines go back to open; a line can be released from a draft with ↩. Legacy timesheets already marked Invoiced by hand are registered as invoiced, so they are never billed again.', 'Amounts are masked for Golden A/S roles without financial visibility; owners never see the register or the reports.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'contacts', 'title' => 'Contacts, companies and customers', 'body' => [
      'Contacts of a sub-project (sub-project › Contacts tab › Select from the vessel\'s contacts): tick the people of the vessel in relation with this sub-project and give each a role for it (approves drawings, yard coordinator …). The e-mail cards of its service and inspection reports tick them first (✉ E-mail button next to the PDF; the other vessel contacts and the owner\'s portal accounts stay available), and the tab has an "E-mail the sub-project contacts" link.',
      'Invoicing details of a vessel (vessel page › Invoicing tab, financial roles): the billing identities of the vessel — owning company, SPV, management company, yard — one default, each with its VAT %, payment terms and bank details. The sub-project form selects the identity the sub-project is billed to (default: the vessel\'s default, else the customer\'s); it shows on the sub-project head and will drive the proforma invoices (release D2).',
      'Commercial › Contacts is the people register: every contact with position, category, company, customer, e-mail, phones, birthday and name day, interests, notes, the vessels they belong to (with their role on board), the communication channels they prefer and the lists they agreed to (newsletter, campaigns, events, greetings). Contacts are also shown on the vessel page and on the project page of the vessels they belong to.',
      ['fig' => 'g-contacts.jpg', 'cap' => 'The contact list with filters, bulk actions (administrators) and export'],
      ['fig' => 'g-contact.jpg', 'cap' => 'A contact — details, vessels, dates, notes and invoicing details'],
      'Companies (Commercial › Companies) is the master list of organisations contacts belong to — owners, management companies, yards, class societies, suppliers. A company linked to a portal customer makes its contacts follow that customer. Customers (Settings › Customers, administrators) are the accounts everything is scoped to, with ERP code, address, VAT and contact details.',
      ['fig' => 'g-companies.jpg', 'cap' => 'Companies — list and edit form with invoicing details'],
      ['fig' => 'g-customers.jpg', 'cap' => 'Settings › Customers — master data, linked companies and invoicing details (several per customer, one default)'],
      ['ul' => ['A contact can be linked to the equipment it is responsible for: open the serial number (Fleet › Equipment) › Link contacts — the picker filters the contact list by search, customer, category or "only the contacts of this vessel" and links several contacts at once with their role for that unit. Linked units are printed on the unit\'s history document.', 'Imports from Excel exist for contacts, companies and customers (with invoicing details); the templates are on the import page. Exports produce the same layout, so correcting in Excel and re-importing is the fastest way to complete data.', 'Only administrators export lists, delete contacts in bulk and edit customers.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'assistant', 'title' => 'Assistant — Claude inside the portal', 'body' => [
      'Golden A/S staff have Claude built into the portal (Settings › Assistant, administrators; per person under Portal accounts › ✨ Assistant). On the pages where it is switched on — service reports, tickets, timesheets, weekly notes, offers, budgets, planning, inspection reports — a ✨ button at the bottom right opens the assistant panel, every text box carries a small ✨ (improve) and, when its content is not written in English, an EN helper (translate). The assistant only proposes; you apply each value and save the form as usual.',
      ['ol' => ['Fill the form: paste the yard e-mail, the WhatsApp message or the handwritten notes — or add a photo of them — and press Propose. The panel lists a proposed value for each field it could fill; Apply one or Apply all, check them, then save the form. Fields it could not fill stay empty and are named in a note.', 'Write: pick the field, say what to do (make it a clear service-report description, shorten, formal tone, add the standard remarks…) and press Propose; Replace the field or Copy the result.', 'Translate: the EN helper appears next to a text box as soon as it contains Greek or Cyrillic text; it proposes the English version, keeping technical terms, numbers and layout. Any text box can be translated from the Translate tab as well.', 'Ask: a question about what is on the page — which items are still open, summarise this thread, what is the hours balance. The answer uses only the form and the page headline.']],
      'What leaves the portal: the fields of the form on screen (labels, options and current values, financial figures included), the page headline and the text or photo you give — never the whole database, never passwords, never another customer\'s data. The company API key is stored encrypted on the server and never reaches the browser. Every call is logged (who, page, kind, tokens, cost) and the assistant stops for everybody when the monthly budget set by the administrator is reached; each person is limited to 30 calls per 10 minutes. Settings: switch, key (write-only), model (haiku = fastest and cheapest, sonnet = recommended, opus = strongest), monthly budget in €, token prices for the cost estimate, pages.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'hr', 'title' => 'HR — leave planning and requests', 'body' => [
      'HR dashboard (first page of HR accounts; administrators: Settings › HR › 📊): who is away today and this week (board chips per day), the requests waiting for a decision, contracts expiring within 90 days (red when past), unread employee messages, headcount and the annual-leave totals of the year, and a table of every employee with entitled / carried over / taken / pending / available (green, gold at 3 or fewer, red when negative), automatic or fixed, and the other types already taken this year — sorted by days available so the people with the least leave left come first. Every name opens the employee card.',
      'Leave is requested by the employee, approved by the manager and then by HR, and lands on the Planning board; balances are kept per person and year under Settings › HR. A Golden A/S role can be scoped "Planning only (HR)" (Settings › Golden A/S roles): accounts with that role see Planning, HR requests and the HR settings and nothing else of the portal; they plan absences for every employee (never project work), approve requests and keep the registry. Administrators have the same HR functions in addition to everything else. A role scoped "Employee — own program, leave and HR" sees only My program (the Planning board filtered to himself, read-only), My leave (balances, requests, messages with HR), the manual, notifications, the knowledge base and the account: use it for staff who do not work with projects.',
      'Employee card (Settings › HR › People › 🪪): one card per employee that stays for life — employee code, Latin / Greek name, portal account, department, job title, contract (indefinite term, fixed term, rotation, seasonal, probation, apprenticeship, internship, project contract, other; part-time flag; contract end date for contracts that expire — the People tab warns 60 days before and after), hiring date, prior service at other employers as years + months, marital status, working days, children. Service: the portal computes the service at Golden A/S from the employment periods, adds the prior service and shows the total — the leave engine uses the total. Employment periods: record Hiring (opens a period), Contract change (new type / end date / title) and Termination (reason: resignation, dismissal, end of contract, retirement, mutual, other — closes the period, marks the person as left and deactivates a staff portal account); when the person comes back, Re-hiring opens a new period on the same card, so the history and the documents are never lost. Every event takes one or more documents (PDF, images, Word, 15 MB each: contract, hiring form, termination notice, ΕΡΓΑΝΗ form, identity / permit, certificate, other) and more can be added to the card at any time; documents are stored outside the web root and opened only by HR / administrators and the employee himself. The last event can be deleted while it has no documents. Personnel register import: the Excel template (People › Excel template) now carries employee code, job title, contract type, contract end, prior service years + months, marital status and leaving date; unknown departments are created on import.',
      ['ol' => ['People (Settings › HR › People) — filter row above the list: free search (Latin or Greek name, employee code, e-mail, job title), discipline, employed / left / everyone, technical or not, employment kind, country, contract type, with or without portal account; the counter shows matches / total and Clear resets.',
      'People (Settings › HR › People): every Golden A/S employee, with or without a portal account — Latin name, Greek name (as in the accountant\'s files), the linked portal account, department, Technical staff flag, hiring date, prior service in months (προϋπηρεσία), 5 or 6 working days a week, children (number and birth years), notes, active. The approver is the one the portal already uses for timesheets — the personal manager of the portal account, else the manager of the account\'s discipline (Settings › Portal accounts / Disciplines) — so nothing is defined twice; the department head (Departments card on the same tab) is only the fallback for people without an account or without a manager on it. Technical staff are the people the project managers see on the Planning board. Departments are the portal Disciplines (Settings › Disciplines) — one structure for accounts, Planning and HR; the discipline manager is the department head. Messages with HR: every employee card has a private thread between HR and that employee (the employee writes and reads it on My leave, HR on the card; both are notified and see an unread badge); nobody else, not even the manager, can read it. Hiring date and prior service feed the statutory allowance engine of the next release (R55); until then entitlements are typed or imported. Bulk entry: Import people from Excel on the same tab — download the template (pre-filled with the current people and the departments), one row per employee (Latin name, Greek name, account e-mail optional, department, technical yes/no, hiring date, prior months, days a week, children, birth years, notes), upload, check the preview (existing people are recognised by Greek name, Latin name or e-mail → update; otherwise create; unknown departments are flagged) and apply — empty cells never erase data. Link the portal accounts afterwards from the person form or by putting the account e-mail in the sheet.', 'Leave types (Settings › HR › Types): code, name in English plus optional names in Greek, Italian, Bulgarian and Ukrainian (each employee sees the type in his portal language; the standard types come translated), kind on the board, counts against the entitlement, reason required, unit (days or hours), HR only (no manager stage — sick, parental, strike, paternity), document required (medical certificate …), paid, consumes annual leave (hour types: every 8 hours = 1 working day of annual leave, used by EARLY Early leave / late arrival), maximum per year (statutory caps such as PAT 14, MAR 5, CAR 5, FMJ 2, SCH 32 h). Built-in types are renamed only; delete only unused ones.', 'Annual leave is computed automatically (Settings › HR › Leave rules, validated with the accountant): in the calendar year of hiring the full-year days pro rata to the days worked; 21 days in the second calendar year, 22 from the third; 25 after 10 years at Golden A/S or 12 years of total service (service at Golden A/S from the employment periods + prior service); 26 after 25 years of total service; 24 / 25 / 26 / 30 / 31 on a 6-day week; carry-over capped (5 days). The Entitlements matrix shows these values greyed with 🔒; on the employee card › Entitlements HR sees per year what the rules give and the basis, and can unlock a year and fix a different value with a reason (🔓 fixed by HR) or return it to automatic — every change is logged. "Create <year> automatically" on the matrix creates the new year for everybody from the rules with the capped carry-over of the previous year. Rule changes are logged on the Leave rules tab.', 'Every other statutory type is computed from the person\'s status as well (card › Entitlements › Other allowances, and the balances on My leave): school attendance 4 days (32 h) per child aged 4–16 from the children\'s birth years; parental leave 4 months (88 working days) per child under 8; child illness 6 / 8 / 14 days for 1 / 2 / 3+ children; single-parent leave 6 (8 with 3+ children) when the card says single parent; carer 5, force majeure 2, marriage 5 (6 on a 6-day week), paternity 14, maternity 119 days, study 30; employer-paid sick leave 13 days below one year of service, 26 above; Italian branch staff (card › Country of employment = Italy) follow the Italian set instead — 20 days annual leave (26 on a 6-day week) with a 30-day carry-over, 104 paid hours (ROL / ex-festività), marriage 15, paternity 10, maternity 150, parental 220 working days per child under 12, child illness 5 days under 8, L.104 disability-care 3 days a month; all values live in Settings › HR › Leave rules › Greece / Italy and must be validated with the accountant and the Italian consulente del lavoro — the law text, not the portal, is the reference. External contractors (card › Employment kind = External contractor, invoice): no statutory leave and no balances — every absence is a request to their manager, whose approval is final (no HR stage), and it goes to the board; they can still use My leave to ask.', 'Gender and leave types per person: the card carries the gender (maternity applies to women, paternity to men; "not stated" keeps both), and the card › Leave types tab lists every type with its default applicability (gender, single parent, L.104, country, contractor) and a switch per type — switch a type off with a note when the person has already used it elsewhere (e.g. paternity leave taken at a previous employer); switched-off types disappear from his request form and balances; every switch is logged. Carry-over: the automatic roll-over carries the untaken annual leave capped by the rule; on the card › Entitlements HR can transfer the untaken days of the previous year (full or any agreed number) into the current year with a note — logged. Leave status strip: every tab of the card starts with the year\'s status — annual leave entitled / carried over / taken / pending / available (green, gold when 3 or fewer, red when negative) and every other type with days or hours left — the HR dashboard per employee; › opens the Entitlements tab.', 'Changes (employee card › Changes): every change on the person with date, who and before / after — card data, entitlements, employment events, documents, request decisions.', 'Entitlements (Settings › HR › Entitlements): per person, year and counting type — days (or hours), carry-over from the previous year and the opening taken days of the year (what was already taken before the portal started counting, e.g. from the accountant\'s Excel). Copy from <year-1> fills a new year, carrying over the remaining days (max 5). The Taken column shows approved requests (+ pending in brackets).', 'Import balances (Settings › HR › Import, one-time at go-live and at each year start if the accountant keeps the sheet): upload the Excel of remaining annual leave (columns name · carry-over · entitled · taken · remaining) and the year; the portal proposes a person for each Greek name (surname first, then first name, ≥ 80 % is pre-selected, lower matches are only suggested), you confirm, skip or create a new person per row, then Apply writes the annual-leave entitlement, carry-over and opening taken days and remembers the matching for the next import. The same person cannot be chosen twice.', 'Import leave records (Settings › HR › Import, second card — go-live and migrations from the old SharePoint list): upload an Excel with a sheet Leaves (Email, Name (Latin), Name (Greek), Type code, From, To, Days, Hours, Status approved / rejected / pending, Reason, Action); people are matched by the e-mail of their portal account, else by Greek or Latin name, types by code; rows already in the portal (same person, type, start date), cancellations and rows marked skip are left out and every row shows its check result before you import. Approved rows are stored as HR-approved requests with the note Import <date> (and land on the board for people with an account), pending rows wait in HR requests. Rows of people who are not in the registry (former employees) can be imported too: tick them and "Create the former employees" — they get a card marked left, without account or balances. Do not count a year twice: when the balances of the year come from the accountant\'s sheet (opening taken), tick History only when importing that year\'s records — they show in the history and on the board but leave the balance as imported; records of other years never touch it.', 'My leave (every employee with a portal account and a person record): balances of the year per type (entitled, taken, pending, available — for EARLY the available hours are the annual-leave balance × 8), a new request (type, dates — or hours and time for hour types — reason when the type requires it, document when the type requires it) and the list of own requests with their stage; a pending request can be cancelled. The portal refuses a request without enough balance, overlapping another request of the same type, on non-working days only, or without the required document.', 'HR requests (managers and HR): managers see the pending requests of their people and approve or reject with a note; HR sees every request, approves at the HR stage (and may decide at the manager stage when the manager is away) with a note and an ΕΦΚΑ / ΕΡΓΑΝΗ reference; HR can also enter a request for a person (paper requests, people without an account). The name in the queue opens the employee card, which has its own Requests section: pending requests are approved or rejected there (note and ΕΦΚΑ reference), with the available balance per request and the last decisions, so HR works from the card without going back to the list. Leave history: pick a person in the filter of HR requests (or 📜 on the People tab) to see every request of all years — type, dates, days / hours, reason, document, decisions, ΕΦΚΑ reference — with the balances of the chosen year, links to the person form and the Planning report, and Print / Save as PDF. Each step notifies the next person and the employee (Notifications › Work).', 'On approval the portal writes the days to the Planning board (one entry per working day with the type, reason and hours) and the board entry carries the request; HR can still plan absences directly on the board as before (counted as taken, without a request).']],
      'HR report (Planning page, HR and administrators only): per person and year — entitled, carried over, taken, pending and remaining per type with OK / Few days left / Over entitlement pills — and the list of absences; All staff gives the yearly matrix. Excel: GAS-HR-<year>.xlsx with a Summary sheet and an Absences sheet. Documents attached to requests are stored outside the web root and streamed only to the employee, the approver and HR.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'mobile', 'title' => 'Mobile app for Service Engineers', 'body' => [
      'The Golden A/S Service app (pilot, build 0.5.0) is the portal on a phone for engineers: it talks to the portal through /customer/api/v1 with the signed-in person\'s own rights, and everything it saves goes through the portal\'s own forms — the same numbering, PDFs, archive, calendars, budgets, accounting lines and notifications as in the browser. Install: open goldensas.com/customer/mobile/ in Safari or Chrome → Share → Add to Home Screen (store builds follow the pilot). Sign in with your portal e-mail and password (and your authenticator code); afterwards unlock with Face ID / fingerprint. HR-scoped accounts and owners cannot use the app.',
      ['ol' => ['Assignments: tickets and works assigned by a PM appear on Home → New assignments; open one, choose the sub-project (or Service call — no project) and Accept.', 'Tickets: Mine / Vessel / All within your branch scope, full history including Freshdesk entries, notes with photos, Start FSR from the ticket.', 'Field Service Report (SF-GLD-014): header with the customer\'s PIC, up to 20 tasks with spare parts (W / G / I), open issues (→ the sub-project\'s issues), up to 3 time lines per task (WT / TT / NC), hours summary, up to 9 photos, customer acceptance in the portal (default) or signature on the phone. Submit queues offline; the portal issues the PDF and DOCX and posts the hours to the accounting register.', 'Inspection report (SF-GLD-020): subject, class / flag, builder / year, hull no., objective, findings, risks, items per vessel system with the portal question bank prefilled, class regulations, risk level, risk analysis and actions → status checking; the PM issues it in the portal.', 'Hours: booked per day into the week\'s portal timesheet of the project with the stage (activity) and section — the calendar reclassifies normal / overtime / holiday, the section budget check applies (an exceeded section asks for the extra-hours request), Submit week sends it to the manager; locked weeks are shown as such.', 'Weekly notes and photos per sub-project (internal or visible to the Owner), project documents (read-only, offers excluded), equipment list with Use in FSR.', 'Claude assistance (✦): the portal\'s assistant on the screen you are on — improve, translate, ask, fill from photo; suggestions only, inserted when you tap Use this text; enabled per page under Settings › Assistant and logged like the browser calls.']],
      'Administration: Settings › Login history › Mobile apps shows active devices, live sign-ins and queued push messages and sets the minimum app version (older builds get "Update required"). Portal accounts lists each person\'s phones with ⎋ Revoke device for a lost or replaced phone; Sign out everywhere also ends the app sessions. Push messages are queued by the portal and sent once the FCM / APNs credentials are configured.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'notifications', 'title' => 'Notifications', 'body' => [
      'The bell counts every change you have not seen: ticket events and comments, work orders, service reports, timesheets, offers, approvals, class submittals, drawing requests, weekly notes, comments, documents and contact notes — plus task reminders (Work group) and, for administrators, licence reminders (Library group). The project manager, administrators and the people on the record receive them; owners receive only what is visible to them. One unseen entry per record per person — a busy ticket is one line.',
      ['fig' => 'g-notifications.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Notifications page for staff'],
      'Calling a colleague with @: in a ticket comment (internal or external), an offer internal note, a sub-project weekly note or comment, an owner-approval comment, a works-forecast reply or a contact note, type @ and pick the colleague from the list (the handle is the part of the e-mail before @ — @np, @ib, @kostas; the arrow keys and Enter choose). The colleague receives the notification "… mentioned you in TK-2026-0012" under Work › Mentions, Open goes straight to the note, and the handle is highlighted in the text. Only Golden A/S staff can be called this way; owners may call them too. Mentions on offer notes reach only roles that see financial data.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'settings', 'title' => 'Settings (administrators)', 'body' => [
      'The Settings page groups its tabs in six categories: My account · Organisation (portal accounts, roles, disciplines, working hours, branches) · Customers & fleet (customers, vessel duplicates, vessel systems) · Lists & categories (offer statuses, task types, issue categories, file categories, e-mail templates, rate cards, offer texts) · Data & imports (Excel, Freshdesk) · System (login history, about / versions, licence).',
      'The gear opens Settings. Account is for everybody; the other tabs are for administrators: Vessel systems (the master list of systems with abbreviation and drawing code, and the Deck photos opacity used under the working areas), Golden A/S roles (name, description, financial visibility), Disciplines (with the manhour rates per hour type), Working hours (calendars and holidays per country), Branches (the head office and the Italian branch — identity, document prefix, offer series, logo), Offer statuses, Portal accounts (create, disable, reset 2FA, working calendar, filters for 2 000+ accounts), Customers, Task types, Issue categories, File categories, Freshdesk import, Login history, About / Versions, Licence, and Import from Excel.',
      ['fig' => 'g-users.jpg', 'cap' => 'Portal accounts — counters, filters, paging, and the add form'],
      ['fig' => 'g-roles.jpg', 'cap' => 'Golden A/S roles with the Financial data — visible switch'],
      'A Golden A/S role whose Financial data switch is off hides more than the amounts: the people in that role do not see Offers at all — no Commercial › Offers entry in the menu, no offer tile on the dashboard (a Tasks tile takes its place), no offer trees, counters or “+ New offer” on the projects and sub-project pages, no offer field on the task form, no Offer statuses tab and no offer import; opening an offer address by hand returns them to the dashboard with a notice, and offer notifications are not sent to them. Owners always see their own offers.',
      ['fig' => 'g-import.jpg', 'cap' => 'Import from Excel — vessels, project structures, vessel systems, offers, contacts, companies, customers, portal accounts; always "Check only" first'],
      ['ul' => ['"N online" in the top bar counts everyone signed in and connected: an open portal page reports every minute, so a person stays online while the portal is open in a browser, drops out about three minutes after closing it and at once on sign-out or idle time-out. Login history lists them by name.', 'A stage that does not exist for a sub-project (no FAT for a pure installation job, say) is ticked N/A on the Process tab: it is drawn hatched on the process line and left out of the overall percentage, the dashboard, the weekly and monthly reports and the vessel history, so the sub-project can reach 100 % without it.', 'Portal accounts export to Excel (Settings › Portal accounts › Export to Excel) in the same layout the import reads — Working calendar, Branch and Branch scope included — so the list can be corrected in Excel and loaded back; the Password column comes back empty and is only filled to reset a password.', 'The Vessels list filters by Projects: with active projects (the working fleet), with any project, or without projects; the Projects column shows the total and the active count.', 'Long lists — projects, vessels, offers, tickets, work orders, owner approvals, companies, timesheets, service and inspection reports, contacts, portal accounts — show 25 rows per page with a pager under the table (25 / 50 / 100 per page); totals, KPIs and filters always cover the whole list.', 'The project code in the breadcrumb of a sub-project, report, offer, approval, timesheet, drawing or work-order page links back to that project on the project list — opened and scrolled into view.', 'Create a customer account: Portal accounts › Add — role Customer, the customer, name, e-mail, a first password (10+ characters) and whether 2FA is required.', 'Create a staff account: role Golden A/S, the Golden A/S role, the discipline, the manager who approves timesheets and the working calendar (GR / IT) that classifies the person\'s hours. Tick 🔧 May prepare work packages for anyone who should create and assign work packages on every project and open Work › Workload (managers, sales engineers, team leads); administrators, the project manager of a project and planning editors can anyway.', 'Welcome e-mail: the ✉ Welcome e-mail button on an account\'s row opens your e-mail program with a ready message in that person\'s language — the portal address, how to sign in the first time, how to change the password and how to enable 2FA. Give the temporary password separately (phone or a second message), never in that e-mail. The text is the E-mail template "Welcome — first connection" (Settings › E-mail templates), with {name}, {email} and {url} for the account.', 'Locked accounts: five wrong passwords lock an account for 15 minutes and every administrator gets a bell notification (Library group) naming the account and the address it came from; the row shows a red Locked pill with a 🔓 Unlock button that clears the lock at once and closes the notification. A lock on an account whose owner did not try to sign in is a sign of someone guessing passwords — check Login history.', 'Sign out with the ⇥ icon at the far right of the top bar (or the button at the bottom of the menu).', 'Disable rather than delete when someone leaves; the history stays.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'archive', 'title' => 'File archive, versions and integrity', 'body' => [
      'Settings › About / Versions shows the portal version, every patch with its database step and whether it was applied, a file-by-file integrity check of the server against the shipped manifest, and the File archive card. Every stored file sits in storage/uploads under customer › project › sub-project › tab with its document number, revision and title in the name; a SHA-256 checksum is kept per file.',
      ['fig' => 'g-about.jpg', 'cap' => 'About / Versions — version, patches, integrity'],
      'The categories a file can be filed under, their document codes and archive folders are configurable in Settings › File categories (see Configurable lists).',
      ['fig' => 'g-archive.jpg', 'cap' => 'The File archive card — Reorganise (check / apply / undo), Verify, Manifest, sizes'],
      ['ol' => ['After a patch: run the database steps shown as Missing, then Ctrl+F5.', 'Reorganise — check only lists every file that is not yet in its archive place; apply moves them (undo within 30 days).', 'Verify (sample / all + orphans) confirms every registered file exists and matches its checksum.', 'Write manifest refreshes storage/uploads/_manifest.csv — the list a backup relies on.', 'Re-file on a project\'s edit page moves its files after a code or name change.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'vessels', 'title' => 'Vessels', 'body' => [
      'Fleet › Vessels lists every vessel with customer, projects and open tickets; staff can add and edit vessels, assign them to a customer, and open the technical history and the weekly report. Vessels usually come from the Excel / SoftOne imports; unknown customers are created automatically and completed later in Settings › Customers.',
      ['ul' => ['Filter the list by Projects — with active projects (the working fleet), with any project, or without projects; the Projects column shows the total and the active count.', 'The Equipment tab of a vessel lists the registered units on board (serial number, maker, model, warranty, last service); the vessel technical-history report prints them with their service history.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-vessel.jpg', 'cap' => 'A vessel page for staff'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'vessel_unify', 'title' => 'One record per vessel — duplicates, aliases, related companies', 'body' => [
      'Imports from SoftOne and Freshdesk entered the same yacht in several spellings (M/Y GIPTA, GIPTA, Μ/Υ GIPTA) and once per customer company, so a project, its contacts, its IMO and its tickets could sit on three records of the same hull. Since v1.30.0 a vessel exists once: the owning company is its customer, the management company, yard, agent and contractors are its related companies (with a role), and every other spelling is an alias. Imports match on IMO, then alias, then the normalised name (prefix, brackets, punctuation, case and Greek look-alike letters ignored) before they create a vessel, and the Freshdesk companies list no longer creates vessels at all.',
      ['ol' => ['Settings › Vessel duplicates lists the suggested groups — same name once the prefix and brackets are ignored, or a bracketed former name equal to another record — with what each record holds (projects, tickets, contacts, equipment, documents) and where it came from. The record with IMO, projects and contacts is proposed as master.', 'Untick anything that is in fact a different vessel, choose the master, correct the proposed canonical name (M/Y NAME) and press Merge into master: projects, sub-projects, tickets, contacts, equipment, documents, tasks, planning entries and the report headers move to the master in one transaction; the master takes the IMO and yard it lacked; the duplicate\'s customer becomes a related company when it differs; the former name in brackets is kept in Former names; the duplicate is retired and its name becomes an alias.', 'A retired record never lists again, but an old link or e-mail that opens it lands on the master.', 'Merge map: upload the reviewed unification workbook (sheet "Merge map (for import)") — Check shows the plan, Apply merges every group in one go.', 'Merge two records handles a pair the groups did not catch (a typo, a renamed yacht).']],
      ['fig' => 'g-vessel-merge.jpg', 'cap' => 'Settings › Vessel duplicates — merge map, merge two records, the suggested groups and the retired duplicates'],
      ['fig' => 'g-vessel-related.jpg', 'cap' => 'Related companies of a vessel with their role, former names and aliases'],
      ['ul' => ['Vessel page › Related companies: the companies behind a vessel with their role (owner, management company, yard, agent, contractor, charterer); the owner stays the vessel\'s customer.', 'Vessel form: Former names (searched like the name) and Aliases (one per line — the spellings SoftOne, Freshdesk or the yards use). The name is normalised on save to <prefix> <NAME>, and a second record of the same hull is refused with a link to the existing one.', 'Naming rule: M/Y, S/Y, M/V, M/T, P/S or HSC, one space, the name in capitals, no brackets — the former name goes in its own field.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'forecast', 'title' => 'Decks, working areas and the weekly works forecast', 'body' => [
      'Every vessel can carry its decks: one photo or general-arrangement drawing per deck, on which the project managers, managers and administrators draw the working areas as rectangles (drag on the photo, name, class) — Interior, Exterior, Machinery, Technical, Guest area, Crew area. Areas without a drawing are kept as a list entry (a tank, a mast, a space with no plan yet). Open the tool from the vessel page › Decks & working areas; a rectangle is stored as a share of the picture, so the plan can be replaced without redrawing. The photo is shown faded under the rectangles so the areas stand out — administrators set the opacity (10–100 %, default 60 %) in Settings › Vessel systems › Deck photos; it applies to the pages and to the forecast documents.',
      ['fig' => 'g-decks-tool.jpg', 'cap' => 'Decks & working areas — the deck photo with the rectangles and the area list with classes'],
      'The weekly works forecast (GAS-WF-<project>-<week>) is the project\'s statement of where the team will work next week: open it from the project\'s action row (Weekly works forecast — the coming week, or ‹ › to another week). A new week starts as a draft pre-filled from the previous week (rolling), the PM edits and issues.',
      ['ol' => ['Plan A — the areas we intend to work in: click an area on the deck map (or + Area to Plan A), tick the sub-projects that work there, write the short works description, tick the days. Plan B — the alternative areas if a Plan A area is not available (Shift-click on the map or + Area to Plan B).', 'Save draft as often as needed; the page shows the deck maps shaded (solid = A, dashed = B), the area tables and the week grid (areas × days).', 'Issue R0: the PDF and Word are archived under the project, the owner\'s users and the project staff are notified, the forecast is locked. Re-open (new revision) returns it to draft; Issue again gives R1 — the owner always sees the last issued revision.', 'The owner replies on the forecast page: a comment to change the schedule, or a delay foreseen, an obstacle in a space, painting in an area — with the area and the dates. Every reply arrives in the bell; delays, obstacles and painting are included in the final report by default (untick what should stay out), a summary line records how the schedule was adapted, then the next revision is issued.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-wf-forecast.jpg', 'cap' => 'The forecast page — deck maps with Plan A solid and Plan B dashed'],
      ['fig' => 'g-wf-editor.jpg', 'cap' => 'The draft editor — plan, area, sub-projects, works, days per row'],
      ['fig' => 'g-wf-replies.jpg', 'cap' => 'Owner replies — kind, area, period; ticked ones go into the report'],
      ['ul' => ['The report: cover block, deck maps with the week\'s rectangles burnt in, Plan A and Plan B tables, the week grid, the owner\'s remarks carried into the final schedule, notes — PDF and Word with the colour code. The weekly vessel report adds a section “Works forecast for next week” when the next week\'s forecast is issued.', 'Who may edit: administrators, accounts with the Project Manager role, and the PM of an active project of the vessel. Owners see issued forecasts of their projects and reply; they never see drafts.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-wf-report.jpg', 'cap' => 'The issued forecast — page 1'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'remote', 'title' => 'Remote support contracts (IT & Cybersecurity)', 'body' => [
      'Library › IT & Cybersecurity holds, besides the cyber documents, the remote support contracts of the fleet — one per vessel and period, with the hours included, the agreed response time, the coverage hours (office, extended, 24/7), how we connect (VPN, TeamViewer on the IAS PC, 4G router), the fee and billing, the renewal mode and the notice period. The number is GAS-RS-yyyy-nnn unless the customer\'s own contract number is typed. The owner sees the contract on his side (status, period, hours used, response time) unless it is marked internal.',
      ['fig' => 'g-rs-list.jpg', 'cap' => 'IT & Cybersecurity — the contracts with period, hours used, response time, tickets and status; the row accent follows the expiry'],
      ['ol' => ['Tickets: every ticket raised on a vessel with a live contract is covered by it automatically (also when a ticket is moved to the vessel); the ticket page shows the contract card with the hours used, and Log session records the remote work (date, hours in quarter-hours, remark) against the contract from the ticket itself. The contract page lists all tickets covered.', 'Tasks: saving a contract with an end date creates — and keeps in step — one renewal reminder task (kind Remote support, high priority, due the notice period before the end, reminded a week ahead, placed on the vessel\'s active project when there is one); + New task on the contract page opens a task already linked to the contract, and the contract page lists its tasks.', 'Hours: sessions logged from tickets or from the contract page add up to the hours used; the bar on the contract page turns amber at 80 % and red at 100 % of the hours included.', 'Expiry: an active contract past its end date is marked expired automatically; the list filter Expiring soon and the badge on the page show what ends within the notice period; the vessel page carries the live contract card.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-rs-contract.jpg', 'cap' => 'A contract — details, hours bar, remote sessions, tickets covered and the renewal task'],
      ['fig' => 'g-rs-ticket.jpg', 'cap' => 'The contract card on a covered ticket with Log session'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'engineering', 'title' => 'Stage sections — budgeted manhours, deliverables and extra-hours approval', 'body' => [
      'Every sub-project has a Sections tab: one card per hour-budgeted section of each stage of the process line — Engineering (Electrical, Automation, Mechanical, UI/UX, IT), Production, Field & Installation, HAT / SAT and Commissioning. The sections are fed by the disciplines mapped to them in Settings › Stage sections: a section\'s budget is the budgeted hours of its disciplines in the sub-project\'s Budget & actual (the agreed budget, by revision), its actual hours are the hours of the same disciplines booked on that stage. One source of truth: the budget is never typed twice and approved extra hours never change it — they are shown over it.',
      ['fig' => 'flow-engineering.svg', 'cap' => 'A section — budget, deliverables, earned value and the extra-hours approval loop'],
      ['ol' => ['Mapping (administrators): Settings › Stage sections — a discipline × section matrix. Tick the sections each discipline feeds: an engineering discipline feeds one engineering section; an installer feeds Field & Installation and HAT / SAT; a service or automation engineer HAT / SAT and Commissioning; production feeds Production. Management and sales disciplines stay unmapped. Sections can be added, renamed, moved to another stage or retired.', 'Budget: enter the hours of the disciplines in Budget & actual (Edit budget R0, R1…) as agreed with the customer — a discipline that feeds several sections gets one cell per section (its row total is the discipline budget, so the live result and cost control do not change). The Sections tab reads the figures. The PM, the section lead, management or an administrator names a section lead per section; the same people manage the deliverables.', 'Deliverables: list the drawings, calculations, programmes, test records, commissioning reports… of the section, each with the hours it stands for (its weight). Tick a deliverable when it is finished. Done % of a section = earned hours ÷ allowed hours, where earned = the weight of finished deliverables scaled to the allowed hours (without deliverables, earned = hours booked, capped) and allowed = budget + approved extra. Every stage with budgeted sections is fed automatically on the process line (weighted by allowed hours) and replaces the production-order evidence for that stage — the PM can still unlock a stage on the Process tab.', 'Booking hours: on the timesheet every line carries the stage it was worked on — the engineer sees the stages of his own discipline plus Other site / service (which feeds no section). The hours count against the section of that discipline and stage. The portal refuses a timesheet that would carry a section beyond budget + approved extra: the engineer sees the message, the PM is notified once.', 'Extra hours: from the Sections tab, Request extra hours — section, hours, cause (misjudgement, miscalculation, scope change, rework, other) and the reason. Management users and administrators decide on Work › Engineering requests (or under the sub-project) with a note; on Production, Field & Installation, HAT / SAT and Commissioning the PM of the project decides as well. The requester and the PM are notified. Approved hours raise the allowed hours and the percentage is recalculated with them included, but the budget stays as agreed: the card shows Used % of the budget (above 100 % = over budget, in red), the hatched part of the bar is the approved extra, Budget & actual shows +N h approved under the discipline, and the section keeps the Budget extended +N h indicator.', 'Finishing early: when a section is complete with hours to spare, Declare finished — its progress becomes 100 % and the remaining budget is released; if deliverables are still pending a note is required. Reopen cancels the declaration.', 'Indicators: On budget (green), Near budget from 90 % of the allowed hours (amber), Budget extended (amber, extra approved), Approval pending (amber), Exceeded (red — hours above the allowed total) and Finished. The project tree shows the same pill next to the sub-project name, and Work › Engineering requests lists every section to watch.']],
      'Permissions: the Management flag on Portal accounts marks who approves extra hours on every section; PMs approve the site stages of their own projects; administrators always can. Owners never see the Sections tab.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'production', 'title' => 'Production orders and production cards', 'body' => [
      'Fleet › Equipment › Production follows what we build in the shop — a switchboard, a console, a panel set — from the work package that asked for it to the as-built. A production order (GAS-PO-2026-001; GIT-PO-… for the Italian branch) belongs to a project / sub-project and a vessel, names its production manager, and lists its units: every serial number becomes a record in the Equipment register (maker Golden A/S, sold by us, on the vessel of the order) with its own production card — the status (not started, in production, tested / FAT, delivered, installed / HAT, commissioned, accepted / SAT) and the percentage finished, set by the production manager. The order\'s own % is the mean of its units.',
      ['ol' => ['Create it from the Works tab of the sub-project (🏭 + Production order next to the work package; also on the work-package page) or from Production › + New production order: project, sub-project, work package, title, system, vessel, production manager, planned dates, the first serial numbers (one per line, maker / model / type / set) or existing units of the vessel.', 'Phases: Design → In production → FAT → Delivered → HAT → Commissioning → SAT → Closed (as-built); Cancelled. Move the order from its page (with a note); Delivered stamps the delivery date and puts the units on the vessel, the later phases pull the units\' status along, Closed sets every unit to 100 %.', 'Units: the production manager (Settings › Portal accounts › Production manager), the order\'s manager, the project manager, work-package managers and administrators set status, % and a note per serial number and save line by line; units can be added or removed at any time.', 'Documents and photos per phase: draft drawings, approved for production, as-built drawings, FAT, production photos, delivery (packing list, delivery note), HAT, commissioning, SAT, other. Upload with document number and revision (D1 / R0 / AB suggested), for all units or one serial number, internal or external (drawings approved / as-built and the test records default to external so they reach the owner\'s Files tab); a drawing already on the project\'s Files tab is linked without a second upload. Every file is also filed on the project and in the archive.', 'Monitoring: the Production tab lists every order with vessel, project / PM, units (accepted ✓), production manager, planned dates (late in red), phase, % and the last update; filters by phase, PM, production manager, vessel and search by serial number. The Equipment register shows the open-orders counter on the tab; a serial number\'s page shows its production card (milestones, status, %, documents, photos) with a link to the order. The production-card PDF (<order>-PC) prints the order, units, documents and log.', 'Notifications: the production manager is notified on assignment; the PM, the manager and the creator when the order moves phase, when a unit is tested / delivered / accepted and when approved / as-built drawings or test records are attached (bell › Work › Production).', 'Stages fed from production: as soon as a sub-project has a production order, every stage of its Process tab is computed from its orders — Engineering from the drawings (none 0, draft 30, approved for production 70, as-built 100; at least 70 once the order left Design), Procurement from the subcontract lines, Production from the serial numbers (production cards), FAT from the units tested (100 when FAT records cover every unit), Installation from the units installed, SAT / HAT from the units commissioned (100 with HAT / SAT records), Commissioning from the units accepted (100 with commissioning reports). The order page shows the rules, the automatic values and an Override column where the production manager can type a figure (blank = automatic); several orders on one sub-project are averaged weighted by their number of units. A stage without evidence keeps the project manager\'s value. On the Process tab a fed stage is read-only (gold) with 🏭 and the orders under it; the PM can Unlock it to type his own value (logged on the order, the feed stops for that stage) and Lock it back. Fed stages carry 🏭 in the process line and the project tree, and * in the weekly report.', 'Subcontracting (external production): on the order, 🔗 Subcontracting records what is built outside — the whole equipment of a serial number (tick "whole equipment") or a part of the work (cabinet fabrication, painting, harnesses, software): supplier (free text or a company from Contacts), our PO number, ordered / due / delivered dates, value (financial roles), status Planned → Ordered → In production (outside) → Delivered to us → Inspected → Accepted (or Rejected / Cancelled) with the % following the status, and the incoming-inspection note. A whole-equipment line drives the production card of its serial number (status In production, % of the line) until it is accepted (unit Tested). Documents of the supplier (PO, drawings, delivery notes, inspection) go to the phase Subcontracting. Production › Subcontracting lists every line across orders — open lines, out at suppliers, arrived / in inspection, late — with filters by status, supplier, PM, vessel, search by PO or serial number, the total value and the Subcontracting report PDF (GAS-SC-YYYYMMDD, grouped by supplier). The PM is notified when a line is delivered, rejected or accepted.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'equipment', 'title' => 'Equipment register — serial numbers and service history', 'body' => [
      'Fleet › Equipment is the register of the units we look after by serial number — shore converters first of all, but also UPS, drives, switchboards and any other unit — each one linked to its vessel (or kept as a site when the unit is ashore) and carrying its own service history: the records imported from the maker lists, the records written automatically from the field service reports, and manual records. Open a vessel and its Equipment tab lists what is on board; open a serial number for the unit data (maker, family, model, sold by us, warranty, first attendance, contacts on board), the other units of its set, the parts replaced and the timeline of every intervention.',
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment.jpg', 'cap' => 'The equipment register — counters, filters, import and export'],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-sn.jpg', 'cap' => 'A serial number — unit data, the set, parts replaced and the service history'],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-pdf.jpg', 'cap' => 'The service-history document of a serial number (PDF)'],
      ['ol' => ['New unit: serial number, set (units delivered together), type, maker and family, model, vessel or site, sold by us with the date, warranty until and reference, first attendance, contacts on board and internal notes.', 'Service history: + Add record writes a dated record (commissioning, maintenance, health check, repair, warranty, upgrade, troubleshooting, inspection, other) with the engineer and the parts replaced; the tabs filter by source. Records written from a service report are read-only here — correct the report and they follow.', 'Field service reports: the Equipment serviced box of the report lists the units of the vessel — tick the ones the report concerns, or type new serial numbers to create them on the vessel. When the report is submitted, its tasks, open issues, parts and engineers are written to the history of every ticked unit (the type is guessed from the text); re-opening the report removes the entries until it is submitted again.', 'Import maker list: the Excel export of the maker database (SN · Vessel Name · 1st Attendance · Vessel Contacts · Service Records · Model · SoldByUs · Date Sold · Warranty Expiration · Retired · Retirement Date) with the maker chosen once for the file. Sets such as "5433-001/002/003" or "4799-001-002-003" become one unit per member, every "dd/mm/yyyy ---" paragraph of the Service Records a dated record, families (SPA II, Classic I/II/III, UHF, SP200, Ultra, Elite …) are read from the model, and the vessel names are matched to the fleet — "M/Y" names not found are created without a customer, other names are kept as a site. Run "Check only" first; a re-import replaces the imported records and leaves the manual and report records alone.', 'Export Excel writes the register with the filters applied — one row per unit with vessel, status, warranty and record count.', 'Print the history: the History PDF and History Word buttons of a serial number issue the service-history document (GAS-EH-<serial>-<date>): unit data, the set, the parts replaced and every record newest first — for the owner, the class surveyor or the maker. The vessel technical-history report (Fleet › Vessels › Technical history) now carries two more tables: Equipment on board and Equipment service history.', 'Makers other than shore converters: the import takes any maker list in the same layout — for AVENTICS / Emerson (Marex OS III, MPC, control heads Type 230 / 240 / 244, ECS) the type is set to Propulsion control and the families are read from the model. Rows without a serial number are registered as "NO-SN <vessel>" with a warning in the import report — edit the unit and type the number when it is known; a record without a date takes the first attendance or the sale date.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-fsr.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Equipment serviced box of a field service report'],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-edit.jpg', 'cap' => 'The unit form'],
      ['ol' => ['Inspection reports: the Equipment inspected box of an inspection report works like the one of the service report; when the report is issued, one inspection record (findings, risk, corrective actions) is written to each ticked unit, and the unit page lists the reports that concern it.', 'Tasks: a task carries an Equipment concerned box — the units of the project\'s vessel (choose the project first). When the task is marked done, a service record (type guessed from the title, otherwise Maintenance) is written to each unit with the assignee as engineer; re-opening removes it. The unit page lists its tasks and the + New task button opens a task already linked to the unit.', 'Linked contacts: on the unit page, Link contacts opens a picker over the portal contacts — search, customer and category filters, "only the contacts of this vessel" — tick one or several, give the role they have for this unit and link them in one go (already linked contacts are greyed); the linked contacts are printed on the history document and the owner sees them on his side. The free-text Contacts on board field remains for people who are not in the contacts list.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-links.jpg', 'cap' => 'A serial number with linked contacts, its inspection reports and tasks'],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-contacts.jpg', 'cap' => 'The contact picker — filters, multiple selection, one role for the batch'],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-isr.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Equipment inspected box of an inspection report'],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-task.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Equipment concerned box of a task'],
      ['ul' => ['Customers see the units on their own vessels — the Equipment tab of the vessel and the serial number pages — with the records of the issued service reports, the imported and the manual records; drafts stay invisible.', 'The counters on top: units in service, under warranty and sold by us; the filters cover maker, family, vessel and status (in service, retired, under warranty, sold by us).', 'Deleting a unit (administrators) removes its records and the links to the reports; the reports themselves stay.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'kb', 'title' => 'Knowledge base and support requests', 'body' => [
      'Searching the help: the search box at the top of Library › Support looks through every section of the manuals you may read (this edition, the Owner edition and the Project Manager handbook) and lists the matching sections with the words highlighted; a result opens the manual at that section with the other sections folded. Inside a manual the filter box above the contents does the same live, as you type — clear it to unfold everything.',
      'Library › Support shows staff the inbox of owner requests (close / re-open after answering by e-mail) and the Knowledge base card. The knowledge base is open to every user: anyone asks, anyone answers, the author (or any staff member) marks the accepted answer, everyone votes answers helpful. Staff can post a question visible to Golden A/S only (internal know-how); administrators delete questions or answers.',
      ['ul' => ['Answer owners\' questions there rather than by e-mail when the answer will serve others — it becomes documentation.', 'Keep confidential figures, passwords and customer-specific commercial data out of it; use the Golden A/S-only visibility for internal technical notes.', 'Categories: Using the portal · Systems on board · Documents & approvals · Service & tickets · Other. Attachments (up to five per post, 20 MB) live under storage/uploads/_kb/Q<id>/ and are deleted with the post.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'tasks', 'title' => 'Tasks and reminders', 'body' => [
      'Work › Tasks is the team\'s to-do list, built like Microsoft To Do: every task has a due date and time, belongs to a project, sub-project or vessel, is assigned to one person or to the project team (project manager + administrators), and can repeat daily, weekly on chosen weekdays, monthly on a day of the month or yearly — every n days / weeks / months / years, with an optional end date. A reminder (at the due time, 15 min … 1 week before) arrives in the notification bell of the assignee or the project team; overdue tasks are reminded again once a day.',
      ['fig' => 'g-tasks.jpg', 'cap' => 'Tasks — Planned view grouped Overdue · Today · Tomorrow · This week · Next 30 days · Later'],
      ['ul' => ['My day — your tasks due today or overdue; Assigned to me — everything open that is yours or your team\'s; Planned — all open tasks; Done — completed ones.', 'The ring on the left completes the task: a repeating task moves to its next occurrence and the completion is written to its history; Skip moves on without a completion.', 'Report reminders (administrators) creates the standing reminders every project needs in one go: weekly report (Fri 15:00), monthly report (1st 10:00), timesheets (Mon 10:00), vessel history (1st 11:00) per active project and a progress update per sub-project (Thu 12:00). Existing ones are never duplicated; edit or delete any of them afterwards.', 'Task types (Weekly report, Monthly report, …) are configurable in Settings › Task types — add your own, colour and order them, hide unused ones; administrators can also create a type from the task form with "+ New type…".']],
      ['fig' => 'g-task-edit.jpg', 'cap' => 'Editing a task — recurrence with weekdays and the history of completions'],
      ['ul' => ['Equipment concerned: once a project is chosen, the box lists the registered units of that project\'s vessel (nothing is listed for a project without a vessel or without units). Marking the task done writes a service record to each ticked unit — the type is guessed from the title, otherwise Maintenance, the assignee is the engineer; re-opening removes the record, recurring tasks keep one record per completed occurrence. From a serial-number page, + New task opens a task already linked to that unit.']],
      ['fig' => 'g-equipment-task.jpg', 'cap' => 'The Equipment concerned box of a task'],
      ['tip' => 'Set the reminder a working day before a report is due, assign it to the engineer who writes it and leave the project manager as a second pair of eyes by keeping a second task for the team.'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'hours', 'title' => 'Working hours, holidays and hour classification', 'body' => [
      'Settings › Working hours keeps one calendar per country — Greece and Italy to start, more can be added. A calendar has the working days with start, end and break per weekday (the normal hours of the day follow from them) and the national holidays of each year: generate the standard list of the country (fixed dates plus Orthodox or Western Easter), import an Excel file (columns Calendar, Date, Name — template on the page) or add single days. Each staff account follows one calendar (Settings › Portal accounts; default GR).',
      ['fig' => 'g-working-hours.jpg', 'cap' => 'Settings › Working hours — weekday hours of the Greek calendar and the holidays of 2026'],
      'When a timesheet is saved the calendar reclassifies the hours: on a working day the first hours up to the daily normal stay normal and the rest become overtime (the line is split); on a public holiday or a Sunday every hour becomes holiday; on a Saturday every hour becomes overtime (per calendar you choose how non-working days count). Hours the engineer already marked as overtime or holiday are kept. Reclassified lines carry an "auto" mark and the save message says how many hours were moved — the approver sees exactly the same.',
      ['fig' => 'g-timesheet-auto.jpg', 'cap' => 'A timesheet after saving — "auto" marks the lines the calendar reclassified'],
      ['ul' => ['Settings › Disciplines carries a cost per hour and hour type for each discipline (normal / overtime / holiday €/h); the Manhours report then shows the manhour cost by discipline for roles with financial visibility.', 'Keep the holiday lists ahead of time: add the standard list for next year before December, and add local or company days by hand or by Excel.', 'An engineer working abroad for a long period can be moved to that country\'s calendar on the Portal accounts tab; the change applies to timesheets saved from then on.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'lists', 'title' => 'Configurable lists: task types and file categories', 'body' => [
      'Two lists that used to be fixed are now yours to shape. Settings › Task types holds the types a task can have (Weekly report, Monthly report, Progress update, …): add your own with a colour and an order, hide the ones you do not use; administrators can also create a type straight from the task form ("+ New type…"). Settings › File categories holds the categories of the Files tab (Drawing, Photo, FAT report, …): each has a document code that goes into the file number (GY-85-02-Y.01-CMR-001) and the archive folder it files into; add, order or hide them. Categories the portal assigns itself (service-report documents, offer documents, issued documents, attachments) are marked system.',
      ['fig' => 'g-task-types.jpg', 'cap' => 'Settings › Task types'],
      ['fig' => 'g-file-categories.jpg', 'cap' => 'Settings › File categories — document code and archive folder per category'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'licence', 'title' => 'Licence and service contract', 'body' => [
      'Settings › Licence shows the serial number, the licensee and the service-contract period of this portal. The key is a signed text block issued by Golden A/S and verified offline — the portal never calls home. Administrators receive bell reminders 60, 30, 14, 7 and 1 days before the contract ends and a banner from 30 days; after the end date the portal keeps working for the grace period (30 days by default) with a red banner, then it locks: every page shows the licence page until an administrator pastes the renewal key. Nothing is deleted while locked.',
      ['fig' => 'g-license.jpg', 'cap' => 'Settings › Licence — contract, installation id and key installation'],
      ['ul' => ['To renew: send the serial number and, for a new installation, the Installation id and the address shown on the page; paste the key you receive into Install a key.', 'A key can limit the number of active accounts and carry the brand name shown in the top bar (white-label installations).', 'A key is refused when its signature is wrong, when it was issued for another address or installation, or when its contract has not started yet.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'conventions', 'title' => 'Numbers and names used in the portal', 'body' => [
      ['ul' => ['Projects: ERP project code (GY-85-02); sub-projects Y.nn (GY-85-02 Y.01).', 'Tickets: TK-YYYY-NNNN. Work orders: <project>-JO-NNNN-YYYYMMDD. Timesheets: TS-YYYY-<initials>-NNN.', 'Service reports: yyyymmdd + initials + hhmm, version R[n]. Inspection reports: ISR-yyyymmdd-<initials>-nn, revision R[n]. Offers: GAS-OF-YYYY-NNN / GIT-OF-YYYY-NNN assigned by the portal at creation, revision R[n]; offers imported from the ERP keep the SoftOne number (ERP pill).', 'Issued documents: GAS-WR (weekly), GAS-PR (monthly), GAS-VH (vessel history), GAS-TS (timesheet statement), revision R[n]; GIT-… when the sub-project belongs to the Italian branch.', 'File categories on the Files tab (and their document codes and archive folders) are configurable in Settings › File categories. Archive files: <document number>_<revision>_<sequence>_<title>.<ext>, Greek transliterated, under customers/<ERP code>_<name>/<project>/<Y.nn>_<name>/<tab>/.', 'Drawings, tests and files get sequences per sub-project (…-DRW-001, …-FAT-001, …-FILE-001).']],
    ]],
  ],
];
